The research stage for Kareem Rahma is done. The result is **ready**, with a standard profile (not an open case). The only file written in the repo is `evidence.json` in the run folder. It passes the pipeline's evidence check, and nothing was published.

**The read: Type 7 at medium confidence. Type 3 is the strongest rival, so the typing is flagged contested and the draft will go to the six-reviewer panel.**

- **For 7:** Asked how he decides what to say yes to, he says "fun, number one is fun" (Death, Sex & Money, 2025). He calls self-entertainment and novelty his deciding rule in five outlets and his own newsletter, and he acts on it in costly ways. When he explains why he wanted money and fame, he names what they buy in experience: "I don't want to say no to fun," and "talent has a very fun job." After his father's funeral he was back at school on the Monday, because "for me, it was like all about motion and momentum." He also writes that "the best part of traveling was the airplane ride."
- **For 3, and why confidence isn't higher:** He made and kept a five-year plan to become famous and framed it as a "rebrand." He says press recognition made him feel "validated" and "really whole," and that he does need validation. His father never gave him "a pat on the back." He calls himself a lifelong social chameleon, set a one-million-follower goal, and ran a deliberate Emmy campaign.
- **Confounds the draft must handle:** He told one podcast he was diagnosed with ADHD as an adult. That can explain jumping between projects, so the type argument rests on his stated motives, not his attention style. The creator economy also rewards constant output and a "do it for fun" persona.

**Sources:** 33 recorded, 32 opened. They include about ten long interviews, ten of his own Substack posts, and two long recordings: the 49-minute Death, Sex & Money episode (I transcribed it locally) and the 85-minute Forbes video (read from YouTube's auto-captions).

**Current facts checked as of 2026-10-25:**
- SubwayTakes was Emmy-nominated in July 2026 and lost to *Colbert Before Air* at the Creative Arts Emmys on September 5–6.
- He is No. 11 on Rolling Stone's 2026 creators list. Net Influencer's "top 10" summary is wrong.
- Keep the Meter Running came back in May 2026 as a long-form YouTube series.

**What the drafting stage needs to know:**
- **Quote checks:** Both recordings are machine text without speaker labels. Re-listen at the timestamps in their locators before quoting. That includes the "really whole" validation passage, which reads as Rahma but needs confirming.
- **Thin testimony:** No friend or collaborator describes his temperament. Co-creator Andrew Kuo talks only about the show, and his wife's views come only through him.
- **Disputed:** His account of the unaired Kamala Harris taping is contested by two campaign officials, so both sides must be attributed.
- **Don't use these unverified or conflicting details:** the June 27, 2026 wedding date (the Vogue story wouldn't open), the Museum of Pizza dollar figures (his own numbers conflict), or Carnegie's "moved at age seven" (his own account says age 3 or 4, in 1989).
- **Privacy:** Don't name his daughter.

I also added a line to my memory notes that Wayback snapshots of NYT articles come back compressed and need unzipping before the text can be read.
